"DW3000 User Manual (https://manuals.plus/m/64aa1e4d95b53cf4ecb1cdc51432f9b9c34748e261b26432895359d9d9527894.pdf)\nciteturn708view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 255; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://manuals.plus/m/64aa1e4d95b53cf4ecb1cdc51432f9b9c34748e261b26432895359d9d9527894.pdf\",\"pattern\":\"PHR rate\"}); Total lines: 11335\nL898@P25: When waking from SLEEP or DEEPSLEEP it is necessary to load the LDOTUNE_CAL value that is programmed\nL899@P25: into OTP during IC production test calibration.Default configuration on power up and after a reset\nL900@P25: DW3000 is a highly configurable transceiver with many features. The register default (reset) values have\nL901@P25: been selected with the intention of minimising the amount of user configuration required. The default\nL902@P25: configuration is summarised in Table 6.\nL903@P25: Table 6: Default DW3000 operational configuration\nL904@P25: Parameter Default Value\nL905@P25: Channel 5 (CF is 6489.6 MHz)\nL906@P25: Data Rate 6.8 Mb/s\nL907@P25: PHR Rate 850 kb/s\nL908@P25: PRF 64 MHz\nL909@P25: Preamble Length 64 symbols\nL910@P25: Preamble Code 9\nL911@P25: Scrambled Timestamp\nL912@P25: Sequence (STS)\nL913@P25: off\nL914@P25: STS Sequence Length n/a\nL915@P25: SFD IEEE802.15.4z [2] length 8\nL916@P25: Channel numbers and preamble codes are as specified in the standard, IEEE802.15.4 standard [1]. Some\nL917@P25: further details are given below on the specifics of the default device configuration. For full details please\nL918@P25: refer to the register map where the default value of each register is given, § 8 – The DW3000 register set.\nL919@P25: Note: the above default configuration needs to be modified in oder for the DW3000 to correctly\nL920@P25: interoperate with the DW1000 on CH5.\nL921@P25: Default system configuration\nL922@P25: Much of the system configuration is configured in the SYS_CFG register, please see section Sub-register\nL923@P25: 0x00:10 – System configuration for a full description of the register contents and defaults.\nL924@P25: By default, interrupt polarity is active high and all interrupts are disabled, see HIRQ_POL in the DIAG_TMC\nL925@P25: register for interrupt polarity and the SYS_ENABLE and SYS_STATUS registers for interrupt configuration and\nL926@P25: information, see sections Sub-register 0x00:3C – System event enable mask and Sub-register 0x00:44 –\nL927@P25: System event status.\nL928@P25: GPIOs are all set to mode 0 (as defined in the GPIO_MODE register), their default function is shown in Table\nL929@P25-26: 7.DW3000 User Manual\nL930@P26: © Decawave Ltd 2019 Version 1.1 Page 27 of 255\nL931@P26: Table 7: GPIO default functions\nL932@P26: GPIO Pin Default Function\nL933@P26: GPIO0/RXOKLED GPIO0\nL934@P26: GPIO1/SFDLED GPIO1\nL935@P26: GPIO2/RXLED GPIO2\nL936@P26: GPIO3/TXLED GPIO3\nL937@P26: GPIO4/EXTPA GPIO4\nL938@P26: GPIO5/EXTTXE/SPIPOL GPIO5\nL939@P26: GPIO6/EXTRXE/SPIPHA GPIO6\nL940@P26: SYNC/GPIO7 SYNC\nL941@P26: IRQ/GPIO8 IRQ\nL942@P26: Frame wait timeout (see SYS_CFG register bit RXWTOE and Sub-register 0x00:34 – Receive frame wait\nL943@P26: timeout period) and preamble detection timeout (see Sub-register 0x06:04 – Preamble detection timeout\nL1414@P39: This can help when constructing a secure ranging environment (see 6 Secure ranging / timestamping) and it\nL1415@P39: also improves immunity to packet collisions on the air.\nL1416@P39: PHR demodulation\nL1417@P39: The main role of the PHY Header (PHR) is to convey the length of the data portion of the packet, and to\nL1418@P39: indicate the data rate being employed for data demodulation. See paragraph 2.9 - PHY header and\nL1419@P39: paragraph 2.10 for details of the PHY header. For data rates of 850 kb/s and 6.8 Mb/s the PHR is modulated\nL1420@P39: / demodulated as per the 850 kb/s data rate (note that because Reed Solomon encoding is not applied to\nL1421@P39: the PHR, its bit rate is approximately 1 Mb/s). If the PHR is indicating 850 kb/s then the data demodulation\nL1422@P39: continues at this rate, but if the PHR is indicating 6.8 Mb/s then the demodulation changes to this rate at the\nL1423@P39: end of the PHR as data demodulation begins.\nL1424@P39: It is also possible to configure the PHR rate to be the same as the data rate, i.e. to use 6.8 Mbit/s with\nL1425@P39: PHR_6M8 configuration bit in SYS_CFG, 8.2.2.4.\nL1426@P39: Data demodulation\nL1427@P39: Section 2.7 – Data modulation scheme describes the modulation scheme. In the receiver a Viterbi decoder is\nL1428@P39: used to recover the data bits (this is also used for PHR reception) which are then passed through the Reed\nL1429@P39: Solomon decoder to apply, if it can, any further corrections. Every octet thus received is passed through a\nL1430@P39: CRC checker that checks the frame against the transmitted FCS.\nL1431@P39: As the data octets are received they may also be parsed by the frame filtering function if enabled, see\nL1432@P39: section 5.4– Frame filtering for more details.\nL1433@P39: Successful reception of a frame is signalled to the host via the RXFR and RXFCG event status bits in\nL1434@P39: SYS_STATUS. Other status bits in this register may be used to flag reception of other parts of the frame or,\nL1435@P39: events indicating failure, i.e. RXPTO (Preamble detection Timeout), RXSTO (SFD timeout), RXPHE (PHY\nL1436@P39: Header Error), RXFSL (Reed Solomon error), RXFTO (Frame wait timeout), etc.\nL1437@P39: Features related to reception Section\nL1438@P39: Receive message time-stamping 4.1.7 – RX message timestamp.\nL1439@P39: Delayed reception 4.3 - Delayed receive\nL1440@P39-40: Receiving long frames 02.10 – Extended PHY headerDW3000 User Manual\nL1441@P40: © Decawave Ltd 2019 Version 1.1 Page 41 of 255\nL1442@P40: RX message timestamp\nL1443@P40: The IEEE802.15.4 standard [1] nominates the time when the RMARKER arrives at the antenna as the\nL2948@P77: This configuration allows selection of PHR type to be one of two options. The default\nL2949@P77: setting gives IEEE 802.15.4 standard [1] PHR encoding and a maximum data payload of\nL2950@P77: 127 octets. The other option enables the long frames mode which allows a data payload\nL2951@P77: of up to 1023 octets. In the latter mode the PHR encoding does not follow the IEEE\nL2952@P77: 802.15.4 standard but instead matches an encoding defined in the IEEE 802.15.8\nL2953@P77: standard. For successful communications between two nodes both must be configured\nL2954@P77: for the same PHR mode. Supported PHR_MODE configurations are:\nL2955@P77: 0 – Standard Frame mode as per IEEE802.15.4 standard [1].\nL2956@P77: 1 – Long Frame mode encoding as per IEEE802.15.8 standard [4], 0-1023.\nL2957@P77: PHR_6M8\nL2958@P77: reg:00:10\nL2959@P77: bit:5\nL2960@P77: This configuration sets the PHR rate to match the data rate when 6.81 Mb/s data rate is\nL2961@P77: used. If this is set to 0, then the PHR will be sent at 850 kb/s. This bit is ignored if other\nL2962@P77: data rate (i.e. 850 kb/s) is selected.\nL2963@P77: SPI_CRCEN\nL2964@P77: reg:00:10\nL2965@P77: bit:6\nL2966@P77: Enable SPI CRC functionality. When this bit is set it enables a Cyclic Redundancy Check for\nL2967@P77: SPI read and write transactions, where SPI write access are assumed to have a CRC byte\nL2968@P77: appended that the IC automatically checks and flags any mismatch (error) in the\nL2969@P77: SPICRCERR, event status bit, and SPI read accesses have a CRC computed internally and\nL2970@P77: available in the SPI_RD_CRC register. The host can then check it against the one it\nL2971@P77: calculates for the read data.\nL2972@P77-78: For more details please refer to see § 2.3.1.3 – SPI CRC modeDW3000 User Manual\nL2973@P78: © Decawave Ltd 2019 Version 1.1 Page 79 of 255\nL2974@P78: Field Description of fields within Sub-register 0x00:10 – System configuration\nL2975@P78: CIA_IPATOV\nL2976@P78: reg:00:10\nL2977@P78: bit:7\nL2978@P78: Select CIA processing of the preamble CIR. When CIA_IPATOV is 1 (default) it configures\nL2979@P78: the CIA algorithm to analyse the preamble CIR and compute an RX timestamp estimate\nL2980@P78: from it. Upon receipt of a packet, the CIA will be automatically run, assuming CIARUNE in\nL2981@P78: Sub-register 0x11:08 – Sequencing control is set to 1 (default). This CIA analysis is started\nL2982@P78: upon SFD detection unless an STS is expected, see § 6 – Secure ranging / timestamping, in\nL2983@P78: which case this processing is delayed until after the STS has been received. When\nL2984@P78: CIA_IPATOV is 0, the CIA algorithm will not run on the preamble CIR. This might be done\nL2985@P78: to save power or processing time when using the STS. If CIARUNE is 0, the CIA_RUN bit, in\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDW3000 User Manual (https://manuals.plus/m/64aa1e4d95b53cf4ecb1cdc51432f9b9c34748e261b26432895359d9d9527894.pdf)\nciteturn708view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 255; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://manuals.plus/m/64aa1e4d95b53cf4ecb1cdc51432f9b9c34748e261b26432895359d9d9527894.pdf\",\"pattern\":\"6.8 Mbps\"}); Total lines: 11335\nL253@P6: • Low external component count\nL254@P6: • Supports enhanced Time-of-Flight security modes\nL255@P6: • Integrated AES CCM* and AES GCM 128/192/256 functionality\nL256@P6: • Worldwide UWB Radio Regulatory compliance\nL257@P6: • Low power consumption (suitable for coin cell battery powered applications)\nL258@P6: • Data rates of 850 kb/s, and 6.8 Mb/s.\nL259@P6: • Packet length from zero to 1023 octets.\nL260@P6: • Integrated MAC support features\nL261@P6: • Up to 38 MHz SPI interface to host MCU\nL262@P6: • Provides precision location and data transfer simultaneously\nL736@P20: The PRF values of 16 MHz and 64 MHz quoted in this document are approximations to the values dictated by\nL737@P20: the IEEE802.15.4 standard [1]. PRF mean values are slightly higher for SHR compared to the PHR and data\nL738@P20: parts of the packet. Please refer to [1] for full details of peak and mean PRFs.\nL739@P20: Data rate\nL740@P20: Where a data rate of 6.8 Mb/s is referred to, this is equivalent to the 6.81 Mb/s data rate in [1]. Note that\nL904@P25: Parameter Default Value\nL905@P25: Channel 5 (CF is 6489.6 MHz)\nL906@P25: Data Rate 6.8 Mb/s\nL907@P25: PHR Rate 850 kb/s\nL908@P25: PRF 64 MHz\nL909@P25: Preamble Length 64 symbols\nL910@P25: Preamble Code 9\nL911@P25: Scrambled Timestamp\nL912@P25: Sequence (STS)\nL913@P25: off\nL914@P25: STS Sequence Length n/a\nL915@P25: SFD IEEE802.15.4z [2] length 8\nL916@P25: Channel numbers and preamble codes are as specified in the standard, IEEE802.15.4 standard [1]. Some\nL917@P25: further details are given below on the specifics of the default device configuration. For full details please\nL918@P25: refer to the register map where the default value of each register is given, § 8 – The DW3000 register set.\nL919@P25: Note: the above default configuration needs to be modified in oder for the DW3000 to correctly\nL920@P25: interoperate with the DW1000 on CH5.\nL921@P25: Default system configuration\nL922@P25: Much of the system configuration is configured in the SYS_CFG register, please see section Sub-register\nL923@P25: 0x00:10 – System configuration for a full description of the register contents and defaults.\nL924@P25: By default, interrupt polarity is active high and all interrupts are disabled, see HIRQ_POL in the DIAG_TMC\nL925@P25: register for interrupt polarity and the SYS_ENABLE and SYS_STATUS registers for interrupt configuration and\nL926@P25: information, see sections Sub-register 0x00:3C – System event enable mask and Sub-register 0x00:44 –\nL927@P25: System event status.\nL928@P25: GPIOs are all set to mode 0 (as defined in the GPIO_MODE register), their default function is shown in Table\nL929@P25-26: 7.DW3000 User Manual\nL930@P26: © Decawave Ltd 2019 Version 1.1 Page 27 of 255\nL931@P26: Table 7: GPIO default functions\nL932@P26: GPIO Pin Default Function\nL933@P26: GPIO0/RXOKLED GPIO0\nL934@P26: GPIO1/SFDLED GPIO1\nL935@P26: GPIO2/RXLED GPIO2\nL936@P26: GPIO3/TXLED GPIO3\nL937@P26: GPIO4/EXTPA GPIO4\nL938@P26: GPIO5/EXTTXE/SPIPOL GPIO5\nL939@P26: GPIO6/EXTRXE/SPIPHA GPIO6\nL940@P26: SYNC/GPIO7 SYNC\nL941@P26: IRQ/GPIO8 IRQ\nL942@P26: Frame wait timeout (see SYS_CFG register bit RXWTOE and Sub-register 0x00:34 – Receive frame wait\nL943@P26: timeout period) and preamble detection timeout (see Sub-register 0x06:04 – Preamble detection timeout\nL944@P26: count) are off, whilst SFD detection timeout (see Sub-register 0x06:02 – SFD detection timout count) is on.\nL945@P26: Other SYS_CFG register settings such as automatic receiver re-enable (RXAUTR) and MAC functions such as\nL946@P26: frame filtering (FFEN), double buffering (DIS_DRXB) and automatic acknowledgement (AUTO_ACK) are all off\nL947@P26: by default. Automatic CRC generation in the MAC frame data is on (DIS_FCS_TX).\nL948@P26: External synchronisation and the use of external power amplifiers are deactivated by default, see sections\nL949@P26: 7.1 – External Synchronisation and 7.2 – External power amplification.\nL950@P26: Default channel configuration\nL951@P26: Channel 5, preamble code 9 and 64 MHz PRF are set by default in the CHAN_CTRL register, see Sub-register\nL952@P26: 0x01:14 – Channel control for more information.\nL953@P26: The transmit data rate is set to 6.8 Mb/s in the TX_FCTRL register, see TXBR field in Sub-register 0x00:24 –\nL954@P26: Transmit frame control . The receive data rate is never set it can be decoded from the PHR bits.\nL955@P26: Default transmitter configuration\nL956@P26: Transmit RF channel configurations are set for channel 5 by default – see Sub-register 0x07:1C – RF TX\nL957@P26: control register 2.The transmit preamble symbol repetition length is 64 symbols, see Sub-register 0x00:24 –\nL958@P26: Transmit frame control, TXPSR field for configuration details.\nL959@P26: Default receiver configuration\nL960@P26: Receiver RF channel configurations are set for channel 5 by default, to match the transmitter.\nL961@P26: Digital receiver tuning registers are configured by default for 64 MHz PRF, 6.8 Mb/s data rate and a preamble\nL962@P26-27: symbol repetition of length 64. See Sub-register 0x06:00 – Digital RX tuning register for programming details.DW3000 User Manual\nL963@P27: © Decawave Ltd 2019 Version 1.1 Page 28 of 255\nL964@P27: The CIARUNE bit (CIA run enable) is enabled by default, which means that the CIA algorithm will execute on\nL965@P27: every packet reception, which in turn will calculate accurate time-of-arrival. If the running the CIA is not\nL966@P27: required then the CIARUNE control in Sub-register 0x11:08 – Sequencing control can be turned off (set to\nL967@P27: zero). This may be useful in a data communications only application, to save power and time.\nL968@P27: 2.6 UWB channels and preamble codes\nL969@P27: The IEEE802.15.4 standard [1] has 16 defined channel/bands for the HRP UWB PHY. The DW3000 supports\nL970@P27: the subset of these listed in Table 8 below. Depending on the channel and the pulse repetition frequency\nL971@P27: (PRF) the IEEE802.15.4 standard [1] HRP UWB PHY defines a choice of two or four preamble codes. The\nL972@P27: standard defined preamble code options are also listed in Table 8. The combination of channel number and\nL973@P27: preamble code is what the standard terms a complex channel.\nL974@P27: Table 8: DW3000 supported UWB channels and recommended preamble codes\nL975@P27: Channel\nL976@P27: number\nL977@P27: Centre\nL978@P27: frequency\nL979@P27: (MHz)\nL980@P27: Bandwidth\nL981@P27: (MHz)\nL982@P27: Preamble Codes\nL983@P27: (16 MHz PRF)\nL984@P27: Preamble Codes\nL985@P27: (64 MHz PRF)\nL986@P27: 5 6489.6 499.2 3, 4 9, 10, 11, 12\nL987@P27: 9 7987.2 499.2 3, 4 9, 10, 11, 12\nL988@P27: The preamble codes specified by the standard for use on a particular channel were chosen to have a low\nL989@P27: cross correlation factor with each other with the intention that the complex channels could to operate\nL990@P27: independently from each other as separate networks. In practice, as there is still some cross correlation,\nL991@P27: there will be some break-through between different codes especially in conditions of close proximity with\nL992@P27: long preambles.\nL993@P27: The IEEE802.15.4 standard [1] includes a feature called dynamic preamble select (DPS), where devices switch\nL994@P27: to using one of the DPS specific preamble codes for the ranging exchange, and perhaps a different one for\nL995@P27: each direction of communication. The idea is to make it more difficult to eavesdrop or spoof, by randomly\nL996@P27: changing the DPS preamble codes in a mutually agreed sequence only known to the valid participants. This\nL997@P27: is supported by the DW3000 where at 64 MHz PRF the preamble codes additionally available for DPS are: 13,\nL998@P27: 14, 15, 16, 21, 22, 23 and 24.\nL999@P27: 2.7 Data modulation scheme\nL1000@P27: The UWB specified in the IEEE802.15.4 standard [1] is sometimes called impulse radio UWB because it is\nL1001@P27: based on high speed pulses of RF energy. During the PHR and Data parts of the packet, information bits are\nL1002@P27: signalled by the position of the burst, in a modulation scheme termed burst position modulation (BPM).\nL1007@P27: this burst phase and use it in a Viterbi decoder to get an additional 3 dB of coding gain, thereby extending\nL1008@P27-28: the operational range of the modulation.DW3000 User Manual\nL1009@P28: © Decawave Ltd 2019 Version 1.1 Page 29 of 255\nL1010@P28: Figure 10: BPM/BPSK data and PHR modulation\nL1011@P28: In addition the quarter symbol interval is sub-divided into 2, 4, or 8 sub-intervals and a pseudo random\nL1012@P28: sequence used to determine both the burst shape and which of the sub-intervals are actually used for the\nL1013@P28: burst transmission. This gives more immunity to interference and whitens the output spectrum allowing a\nL1014@P28: higher signal power to be used in the transmitter.\nL1015@P28: Forward error correction (FEC) is also included in the PHR and Data parts of the packet. The 19-bit PHR\nL1016@P28: includes a 6-bit single-error-correct double-error-detect (SECDED) code and the data part of the packet has a\nL1017@P28: Reed Solomon (RS) code applied. Both SECDED and RS codes are termed “systematic” meaning that the data\nL1018@P28: can be recovered without using the codes (and of course not benefitting from them in that case), e.g. by a\nL1019@P28: non-coherent receiver. The 850 kb/s and 6.8 Mb/s user data rates quoted, include allowance for the 0.87\nL1020@P28: average RS coding rate. The PHR is not RS coded so for example at the 850 kb/s nominal rate the PHR is\nL1021@P28: actually sent at 975 kb/s.\nL1022@P28: 2.8 Synchronisation header modulation scheme\nL1023@P28: The Synchronisation Header (SHR) consists of the preamble sequence and the SFD (start of frame delimiter).\nL1024@P28: In contrast to the BPM/BPSK modulation used for the PHR and data, the synchronisation header is made up\nL1025@P28: of single pulses. The preamble symbol period is divided into approximately 500 “chip” time intervals,\nL1026@P28: (496/508 depending on 16/64 MHz PRF1), in which either a negative or a positive pulse may be sent, or no\nL1027@P28: pulse. The “chip” interval is 499.2 MHz, a fundamental frequency within the UWB PHY, and so the resultant\nL1028@P28: symbol times are thus 496/499.2 µs for 16 MHz PRF, and 508/499.2 µs for 64 MHz PRF, (see Table 9 below).\nL1029@P28: The sequence of pulses sent during the preamble symbol interval is determined by the preamble code. The\nL1030@P28: standard defines 8 preamble codes of length-31 for use at 16 MHz PRF and 16 preamble codes of length-127\nL1031@P28: for use at 64 MHz PRF. The standard nominates particular codes for particular channels so that at 16 MHz\nL1399@P38: demodulation to the BPM/BPSK demodulation of the PHR and data or STS demodulation if enabled.\nL1400@P38: It is possible to abort reception if the SFD is not detected within a certain time after preamble is detected.\nL1401@P38: This functionality is configured via DRX_SFDTOC. This SFD detection timeout guards against false detection\nL1402@P38: of preamble (which has a finite chance of happening) that could otherwise lead to a prolonged period\nL1403@P38: receiving nothing. By default, the SFD detection timeout is 65 symbol times (to match the default preamble\nL1404@P38-39: length of 64 symbols and a PAC size of 8). It is not recommended to disable the SFD detection timeout. DW3000 User Manual\nL1405@P39: © Decawave Ltd 2019 Version 1.1 Page 40 of 255\nL1406@P39: The SFD sequence is either 8 or 16 symbols long for the supported data rates of 6.8 Mb/s or 850 kb/s. The\nL1407@P39: type of the SFD sequence used is defined by SFD_TYPE configuration bits in CHAN_CTRL register, and are\nL1408@P39: described in Table 22.\nL1409@P39: STS reception\nL1410@P39: If the STS is enabled, see Figure 13, the receiver will construct another CIR from that. The time-of-arrival\nL1411@P39: (ToA) can also be derived from this CIR estimate.\nL1412@P39: The STS itself consists of a series of equally spaced pulses whose polarity is derived from an AES128 based\nL1413@P39: cryptographically secure pseudo random number generator as specified in the IEEE802.15.4z amendment.\nL1414@P39: This can help when constructing a secure ranging environment (see 6 Secure ranging / timestamping) and it\nL1415@P39: also improves immunity to packet collisions on the air.\nL1416@P39: PHR demodulation\nL1417@P39: The main role of the PHY Header (PHR) is to convey the length of the data portion of the packet, and to\nL1418@P39: indicate the data rate being employed for data demodulation. See paragraph 2.9 - PHY header and\nL1419@P39: paragraph 2.10 for details of the PHY header. For data rates of 850 kb/s and 6.8 Mb/s the PHR is modulated\nL1420@P39: / demodulated as per the 850 kb/s data rate (note that because Reed Solomon encoding is not applied to\nL1421@P39: the PHR, its bit rate is approximately 1 Mb/s). If the PHR is indicating 850 kb/s then the data demodulation\nL1422@P39: continues at this rate, but if the PHR is indicating 6.8 Mb/s then the demodulation changes to this rate at the\nL1423@P39: end of the PHR as data demodulation begins.\nL1424@P39: It is also possible to configure the PHR rate to be the same as the data rate, i.e. to use 6.8 Mbit/s with\nL1425@P39: PHR_6M8 configuration bit in SYS_CFG, 8.2.2.4.\nL1426@P39: Data demodulation\nL1427@P39: Section 2.7 – Data modulation scheme describes the modulation scheme. In the receiver a Viterbi decoder is\nL1428@P39: used to recover the data bits (this is also used for PHR reception) which are then passed through the Reed\nL1429@P39: Solomon decoder to apply, if it can, any further corrections. Every octet thus received is passed through a\nL1430@P39: CRC checker that checks the frame against the transmitted FCS.\nL1431@P39: As the data octets are received they may also be parsed by the frame filtering function if enabled, see\nL1432@P39: section 5.4– Frame filtering for more details.\nL1433@P39: Successful reception of a frame is signalled to the host via the RXFR and RXFCG event status bits in\nL1434@P39: SYS_STATUS. Other status bits in this register may be used to flag reception of other parts of the frame or,\nL1435@P39: events indicating failure, i.e. RXPTO (Preamble detection Timeout), RXSTO (SFD timeout), RXPHE (PHY\nL1436@P39: Header Error), RXFSL (Reed Solomon error), RXFTO (Frame wait timeout), etc.\nL1437@P39: Features related to reception Section\nL1438@P39: Receive message time-stamping 4.1.7 – RX message timestamp.\nL1439@P39: Delayed reception 4.3 - Delayed receive\nL1440@P39-40: Receiving long frames 02.10 – Extended PHY headerDW3000 User Manual\nL1441@P40: © Decawave Ltd 2019 Version 1.1 Page 41 of 255\nL1442@P40: RX message timestamp\nL1443@P40: The IEEE802.15.4 standard [1] nominates the time when the RMARKER arrives at the antenna as the\nL1444@P40: significant event that is time-stamped, also shown in Figure 13.\nL1445@P40: The DW3000 digital receiver circuitry takes a coarse timestamp of the symbol in which the RMARKER event\nL1446@P40: occurs and adds in various correction factors to give a resultant adjusted time stamp value, which is the time\nL1447@P40: at which the RMARKER arrived at the antenna. This includes subtracting the receive antenna delay as\nL1448@P40: configured in the RXANTD register (in CIA_CONF) and adding the correction factor determined by the first\nL1449@P40: path (leading edge) detection algorithm embedded in the DW3000. The resulting fully adjusted RX\nL1450@P40: timestamp is written into RX_STAMP, IP_TOA and STS_TOA registers.\nL1451@P40: See also section 10.3 – IC calibration – antenna delay, and section 6 – Secure ranging / timestamping.\nL2058@P56: Figure 23: AES engine source/destination buffers\nL2059@P56: AES Configuration\nL2060@P56: Before the encryption or decryption of the data, the AES engine needs to be configured with:\nL2061@P56: • the MIC size (TAG_SIZE), which can be 0, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14 or 16 bytes\nL2062@P56: • the KEY size (128, 192 or 256-bits) and location (KEY can be stored in DW3000 registers, or OTP, or\nL2063@P56: AES KEY RAM). If a KEY from DW3000 registers or AES KEY RAM is used then it needs to be written\nL2064@P56: to AES_KEY register or AES KEY RAM location.\nL4539@P105: has a its W4R_TIM parameter set to 0, the recommended minimum ACK_TIM settings are as\nL4540@P105: follows:\nL4541@P105: Data Rate Recommend min.\nL4542@P105: ACK_TIM\nL4543@P105: 850 kb/s 2\nL4544@P105: 6.8 Mb/s 3\nL4545@P105: This is most important at the 6.8 Mb/s data rate, where preamble sequences are generally\nL4546@P105: short, and losing even a few preamble symbols could potentially compromise ACK reception.\nL4547@P105: Where the W4R_TIM parameter is larger than zero, the ACK_TIM setting should be increased\nL6124@P138: Bits marked ‘-’ are reserved and should be written as zero.\nL6125@P138: 8.2.6.8 Sub-register 0x05:1C– GPIO interrupt mode selection\nL6126@P138: ID\nL6127@P138: Length\nL6128@P138: (octets)\nL6129@P138: Type Mnemonic Description\nL6130@P138: 05:1C 2 RW GPIO_IMODE GPIO interrupt mode (level/edge)\nL6131@P138: Register file: 0x05 – GPIO control and status, sub-register 0x1C is the GPIO interrupt mode selection register.\nL6132@P138: Assuming that the GPIO is an input and enabled as an interrupt via the GPIO_IRQE register, then this\n"
